{"about":"055185d569b6c41588ae3dbf84fd3173f665c1e8755339a3cba55b119c356f87","body":"The charge of 'performative contradiction' assumes that acting on a theory necessitates believing it is probable or true in an inductive sense. However, Popper's framework allows for acting on the best-available hypothesis without assigning it any positive probability value. The decision to use the corroborated bridge design is a pragmatic application of the current state of critical debate, acknowledging that while the untested theory is logically possible, it has not yet earned the status of having survived severe testing. This is a procedural choice within a fallibilist system, not a surreptitious return to the justification of knowledge via induction.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}